In .NET 10 (and late .NET 9), Android's `SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_RESIZE` is fully deprecated and MAUI no longer honors `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize`. The replacement is **`KeyboardAutoManagerScroll`**, which is enabled by default.

### Quick fix

Make sure you haven't disabled it. If your input is still hidden, explicitly opt the page/view in:

```xml
<!-- On your page or the input view -->
<ContentPage
    xmlns:android="clr-namespace:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.PlatformConfiguration.AndroidSpecific;assembly=Microsoft.Maui.Controls"
    android:Application.WindowSoftInputModeAdjust="Resize">
```

Replace with:

```csharp
// In your page or view code-behind / handler
Microsoft.Maui.Platform.KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Connect(this);
```

Or ensure it's not disconnected globally. In `MauiProgram.cs`:

```csharp
// Do NOT call this (it disables the auto-scroll):
// KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Disconnect();
```

### If auto-scroll isn't enough (e.g., custom layouts)

Handle Android window insets directly in a custom handler or platform code:

```csharp
#if ANDROID
using AndroidX.Core.View;

// In your MainActivity or a custom handler:
ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(platformView, (view, insets) =>
{
    var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
    // Adjust bottom padding/margin by imeInsets.Bottom
    view.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, imeInsets.Bottom);
    return insets;
});
#endif
```

### Summary

| .NET 9 (old) | .NET 10 (new) |
|---|---|
| `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` | `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll` (default) or manual `WindowInsetsCompat` handling |

The auto-manager scrolls the focused input into view automatically. If your layout uses absolute positioning or a custom scroll structure, the insets listener gives you full control.
